WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.201 --> 00:00:02.268
-Our next guest
is a very funny comedian

00:00:02.268 --> 00:00:03.936
you know
from "Saturday Night Live"

00:00:03.936 --> 00:00:06.639
as well as movies such as
"Sisters" and "Wine Country."

00:00:06.639 --> 00:00:09.875
She's starring in the upcoming
Broadway Show "POTUS:

00:00:09.875 --> 00:00:11.911
Or, Behind Every Great Dumbass
are Seven Women

00:00:11.911 --> 00:00:13.480
Trying to Keep Him Alive,"
which will open

00:00:13.480 --> 00:00:15.881
at the Shubert Theatre
on April 27th.

00:00:15.881 --> 00:00:17.750
Please welcome back to the show
our very dear friend

00:00:17.750 --> 00:00:19.152
Rachel Dratch, everybody!

00:00:19.152 --> 00:00:20.486
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:20.486 --> 00:00:30.496 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:30.496 --> 00:00:36.336 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:36.336 --> 00:00:38.972
Welcome, Dratch!

00:00:38.972 --> 00:00:41.407
You have --
-Hello!

00:00:41.407 --> 00:00:43.576
-I am already in your debt
in life in general,

00:00:43.576 --> 00:00:45.611
but you did us a favor tonight.

00:00:45.611 --> 00:00:48.114
You showed up as a sort of --
you filled in for

00:00:48.114 --> 00:00:50.683
a last-minute guest hole,
and thank you so much.

00:00:50.683 --> 00:00:53.219
-Yes, I love to take up
a guest hole.

00:00:53.219 --> 00:00:55.888
[ Laughter ]
No, yeah, I got a call

00:00:55.888 --> 00:00:57.257
not too long ago,

00:00:57.257 --> 00:01:00.526
and I like to think of myself
as your Teri Garr.

00:01:00.526 --> 00:01:02.128
-That is a very important role.

00:01:02.128 --> 00:01:04.497
-For those of you
who used to watch "Letterman."

00:01:04.497 --> 00:01:07.100
-Every talk show host needs
their Teri Garr, though --

00:01:07.100 --> 00:01:10.069
someone they can trust,
who can show up, can deliver.

00:01:10.069 --> 00:01:11.770
-Yes.
Now, let me say that

00:01:11.770 --> 00:01:13.973
I was making chili
when I got this call,

00:01:13.973 --> 00:01:16.042
so I was cooking onions,
so I might smell like onions.

00:01:16.042 --> 00:01:17.443
-Okay, that's all right.

00:01:17.443 --> 00:01:19.645
We do appreciate you
putting that caveat out there.

00:01:19.645 --> 00:01:20.913
-Okay, good.
-Hey, you know what

00:01:20.913 --> 00:01:23.449
I was thinking when Mariska
and Chris were out here?

00:01:23.449 --> 00:01:26.019
I think one of my first
or second weeks at "SNL,"

00:01:26.019 --> 00:01:29.788
you wrote a sketch called
"Law &amp; Order: SUV."

00:01:29.788 --> 00:01:31.591
-I did?
-You did.

00:01:31.591 --> 00:01:34.060
-I don't remember
half the stuff I did.

00:01:34.060 --> 00:01:35.661
What was it?
-It was --

00:01:35.661 --> 00:01:37.997
I think it was basically
just SUV-related crimes.

00:01:37.997 --> 00:01:41.400
-Really? I wrote that?
[ Laughter ]

00:01:41.400 --> 00:01:43.235
-Look at you!
-I guess I did!

00:01:43.235 --> 00:01:44.570
-This is one of the new things
we're trying with guests

00:01:44.570 --> 00:01:46.539
where I tell you
something about you.

00:01:46.539 --> 00:01:48.074
[ Laughter ]

00:01:48.074 --> 00:01:50.676
-God,
I don't remember doing that.

00:01:50.676 --> 00:01:52.211
I mean, I remember the sketch.
I didn't remember --

00:01:52.211 --> 00:01:53.546
Okay.
Anyway, let's move on from that.

00:01:53.546 --> 00:01:55.247
-Okay. You, um --
[ Laughter ]

00:01:55.247 --> 00:01:57.350
You might not know this --
you're in a Broadway show.

00:01:57.350 --> 00:01:59.485
-I am!
I do know that.

00:01:59.485 --> 00:02:01.487
-That's so exciting.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:02:01.487 --> 00:02:05.391
Now, I've seen you --
I've seen you onstage before,

00:02:05.391 --> 00:02:06.759
but I'm not sure --

00:02:06.759 --> 00:02:08.394
I did not realize
this is your Broadway debut.

00:02:08.394 --> 00:02:12.665
-Broadway debut, yes.
It's a farce with seven women,

00:02:12.665 --> 00:02:15.234
written by a woman,
directed by Susan Stroman,

00:02:15.234 --> 00:02:17.369
written -- Selina Fillinger
is the playwright.

00:02:17.369 --> 00:02:19.539
She's a young,
up-and-coming playwright

00:02:19.539 --> 00:02:22.275
who got her play on Broadway.
-That's amazing.

00:02:22.275 --> 00:02:24.844
-And it's me and --
I mean, I hope I remember

00:02:24.844 --> 00:02:26.980
everyone right now,
but Vanessa Williams,

00:02:26.980 --> 00:02:29.815
Julie White, Lea DeLaria,
Lilli Cooper,

00:02:29.815 --> 00:02:32.851
Suzy Nakamura from Second City,
our buddy, and --

00:02:32.851 --> 00:02:35.254
oh, my God --
Julianne Hough!

00:02:35.254 --> 00:02:37.022
-Wow!
Nice catch -- you got them all.

00:02:37.022 --> 00:02:38.424
-Yeah.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:02:38.424 --> 00:02:40.493
-That's really exciting.
-Yeah.

00:02:40.493 --> 00:02:44.530
-And this was -- is it safe to
say that even before comedy,

00:02:44.530 --> 00:02:45.998
theater was your thing?

00:02:45.998 --> 00:02:48.234
You were in an early -- you were
in a production of "Music Man."

00:02:48.234 --> 00:02:49.602
-I was, in high school.

00:02:49.602 --> 00:02:50.670
-So, this is
a high school production?

00:02:50.670 --> 00:02:53.907
-That's me in "Music Man"
as Amaryllis.

00:02:53.907 --> 00:02:55.774
-Okay.
-I did have --

00:02:55.774 --> 00:02:57.677
This is from the yearbook,
and I noticed

00:02:57.677 --> 00:03:00.446
I left my watch on by accident.
[ Laughter ]

00:03:00.446 --> 00:03:01.681
So that's kind of anachronistic.

00:03:01.681 --> 00:03:03.816
-That's how it gives away
the con, right?

00:03:03.816 --> 00:03:05.551
[ Laughter ]
-Yes.

00:03:05.551 --> 00:03:07.220
But that was
the dress rehearsal.

00:03:07.220 --> 00:03:08.687
I did take it off
for the real show.

00:03:08.687 --> 00:03:11.024
But anyway, yes, that was me
in "The Music Man"

00:03:11.024 --> 00:03:13.259
in my junior year
of high school, yeah.

00:03:13.259 --> 00:03:16.629
-Amaryllis is the young girl
who's taking piano lessons.

00:03:16.629 --> 00:03:18.497
-Yeah, so it's not a major role.

00:03:18.497 --> 00:03:19.732
-Did you have to do a lot of,

00:03:19.732 --> 00:03:21.601
like, miming playing piano,
do you remember?

00:03:21.601 --> 00:03:23.169
-Well, I actually played
the piano, Seth, so...

00:03:23.169 --> 00:03:25.004
-Oh! Well!
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:03:25.004 --> 00:03:26.772
-I mean, it's just a little --
like, anyone could learn that.

00:03:26.772 --> 00:03:29.876
But anyways, yeah,
so I've done theater a lot,

00:03:29.876 --> 00:03:32.044
but I've always gravitated
towards comedy.

00:03:32.044 --> 00:03:35.247
So, you know,
this is like, whatever.

00:03:35.247 --> 00:03:37.850
This is gonna be fun because --

00:03:37.850 --> 00:03:39.218
-Have you seen "The Music Man"
on Broadway?

00:03:39.218 --> 00:03:40.887
-I did see it, yes.
-Was it exciting?

00:03:40.887 --> 00:03:42.689
-Yeah. Oh, my gosh,
'cause I know all the songs.

00:03:42.689 --> 00:03:45.058
When you go to a show that you
were in, then you're, like,

00:03:45.058 --> 00:03:46.592
singing along in your seat,

00:03:46.592 --> 00:03:48.394
much to the chagrin
of your seatmates.

00:03:48.394 --> 00:03:50.296
[ Laughter ]
But no, yes, I saw "Music Man."

00:03:50.296 --> 00:03:53.065
It was super fun.
-We talked about your son Eli,

00:03:53.065 --> 00:03:55.067
who is now 11 1/2,
which is amazing.

00:03:55.067 --> 00:03:56.836
-Yes, yes.
-I remember seeing him

00:03:56.836 --> 00:03:58.470
in a stroller,
because he wasn't always 11 1/2.

00:03:58.470 --> 00:03:59.839
-Yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:03:59.839 --> 00:04:02.341
-You talked about bringing him
to Broadway shows.

00:04:02.341 --> 00:04:06.245
My wife just brought our oldest
to "Lion King," which he loved.

00:04:06.245 --> 00:04:09.148
You talked about bringing Eli
to "Fiddler on the Roof."

00:04:09.148 --> 00:04:11.551
-I did, yeah.
-And what was his review, again?

00:04:11.551 --> 00:04:13.986
-Well, he said it was boring
and sad and boring.

00:04:13.986 --> 00:04:15.588
-Yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:04:15.588 --> 00:04:17.256
-But so,
I've brought him to more,

00:04:17.256 --> 00:04:19.325
like, kid-friendly musicals.
I don't want you to think

00:04:19.325 --> 00:04:20.860
I threw him in
on "Fiddler on the Roof."

00:04:20.860 --> 00:04:24.330
But as I told you last time
I was here -- whatever --

00:04:24.330 --> 00:04:25.397
yeah, like,
"Fiddler on the Roof" --

00:04:25.397 --> 00:04:26.732
I used to love that --

00:04:26.732 --> 00:04:28.267
Like, I saw it when I was
little, and I really loved it,

00:04:28.267 --> 00:04:29.435
and I wanted him to see it,

00:04:29.435 --> 00:04:31.838
but it didn't go over
so great with him, yeah.

00:04:31.838 --> 00:04:34.674
-Do you think -- Did you --
In the watching it,

00:04:34.674 --> 00:04:36.842
did you realize early on, "Oh,
I've made a terrible mistake"?

00:04:36.842 --> 00:04:39.945
-Yes, yes.
Because it was kind of, like,

00:04:39.945 --> 00:04:42.514
dark sets, and, like,
it wasn't really, like,

00:04:42.514 --> 00:04:44.784
a lot of, you know,
real-life-looking stuff.

00:04:44.784 --> 00:04:47.753
And then, I also realized, like,
"Oh, yeah, this play is kind of

00:04:47.753 --> 00:04:50.289
about, like, marrying someone
outside of your religion."

00:04:50.289 --> 00:04:54.059
He was like 6,
so it wasn't exactly a child --

00:04:54.059 --> 00:04:58.297
but I think -- now I'm just
repeating another segment I did.

00:04:58.297 --> 00:05:01.000
But he did lean over to me
partway through and be like,

00:05:01.000 --> 00:05:03.169
"I wanna go."
[ Laughter ]

00:05:03.169 --> 00:05:05.971
And then one of the characters
onstage said,

00:05:05.971 --> 00:05:07.773
"I have to leave you now,"
and he leans over,

00:05:07.773 --> 00:05:09.675
and he goes,
"I have to leave this show."

00:05:09.675 --> 00:05:11.076
[ Laughter ]

00:05:11.076 --> 00:05:13.846
So, that's a rewind
of another Seth segment.

00:05:13.846 --> 00:05:16.148
-Hey, look, sometimes
when you're Teri Garr --

00:05:16.148 --> 00:05:18.817
-When you're filling in, you got
to go play the hits, right?

00:05:18.817 --> 00:05:20.119
[ Laughter and applause ]

00:05:20.119 --> 00:05:22.121
-So, wait.
Are you gonna bring --

00:05:22.121 --> 00:05:24.090
Will Eli come to this show?
-Okay.

00:05:24.090 --> 00:05:27.993
So this show has a lot,
a lot of salty language in it.

00:05:27.993 --> 00:05:29.962
-Oh, okay.
-Really, really.

00:05:29.962 --> 00:05:31.964
So, I don't know, 'cause
he's heard a lot of swears --

00:05:31.964 --> 00:05:35.567
-How salty?
-Like, think saltiest.

00:05:35.567 --> 00:05:36.902
-Okay.
[ Laughter ]

00:05:36.902 --> 00:05:40.473
-No, he's heard a lot of,
like, bad words,

00:05:40.473 --> 00:05:43.543
but I don't know that I want to
introduce him to new bad words.

00:05:43.543 --> 00:05:44.710
But I don't know.
[ Laughter ]

00:05:44.710 --> 00:05:46.211
We'll see,
'cause then I'm also, like,

00:05:46.211 --> 00:05:48.147
"Well, I want him to see it,
so maybe I'll just, like,

00:05:48.147 --> 00:05:50.582
have a little sit-down
and lay down the --

00:05:50.582 --> 00:05:53.052
-That's so interesting because
I think there's no way

00:05:53.052 --> 00:05:55.621
to stop your kids
hearing the bad words,

00:05:55.621 --> 00:05:57.557
but you don't want it
to be by your hand.

00:05:57.557 --> 00:05:59.592
-Exactly.
And then do you want him

00:05:59.592 --> 00:06:01.961
to go tell everyone in the fifth
grade about the new word?

00:06:01.961 --> 00:06:04.130
Yeah.
-And like, he heard --

00:06:04.130 --> 00:06:06.999
"I know this because I saw my
mom say it on a stage."

00:06:06.999 --> 00:06:09.434
-Well, actually, I don't say it.
-Oh, there you go.

00:06:09.434 --> 00:06:11.503
So maybe
when the night he comes,

00:06:11.503 --> 00:06:13.539
every time someone
says a bad word, you, like,

00:06:13.539 --> 00:06:15.207
do a big thing
of rolling your eyes.

00:06:15.207 --> 00:06:17.510
-Yeah, or I'm like,
"Wasn't me, wasn't me."

00:06:17.510 --> 00:06:18.610
[ Laughter ]

00:06:18.610 --> 00:06:20.546
No, but the show is very funny.

00:06:20.546 --> 00:06:23.148
So, I start out --
Like, the character that I play

00:06:23.148 --> 00:06:26.219
is really fun 'cause I start out
as someone who's very, like,

00:06:26.219 --> 00:06:27.954
buttoned-up and apologetic.

00:06:27.954 --> 00:06:30.723
Like, I can really relate to
that being, like, "Oh, my God.

00:06:30.723 --> 00:06:32.090
did I do this right?"
that kind of thing.

00:06:32.090 --> 00:06:35.027
But then the character
goes on a journey,

00:06:35.027 --> 00:06:36.729
and I get to do
a lot of crazy things.

00:06:36.729 --> 00:06:39.365
So, yeah.
I was thinking,

00:06:39.365 --> 00:06:42.901
if you like me as an actor,
you'll really like this show.

00:06:42.901 --> 00:06:44.604
And then, if you don't like me,

00:06:44.604 --> 00:06:47.206
there's a lot of other ladies
there that you might be fans of.

00:06:47.206 --> 00:06:49.075
[ Laughter ]

00:06:49.075 --> 00:06:51.777
-What a wonderful --
-Put that on the marquee.

00:06:51.777 --> 00:06:54.580
"If you hate Rachel Dratch,
there are six others

00:06:54.580 --> 00:06:56.248
that you might enjoy."
[ Laughter ]

00:06:56.248 --> 00:06:57.750
-Well, I think I'm gonna like
all seven of you.

00:06:57.750 --> 00:06:59.786
Thanks so much for showing up,
for bailing us out.

00:06:59.786 --> 00:07:01.720
It is always such a joy
to see you.

00:07:01.720 --> 00:07:03.289
Rachel Dratch!
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:07:03.289 --> 00:07:06.526
"POTUS" officially opens
on Broadway April 27th.

